}

\Babstract{
The \tcode{std::widening_mul} function proposed in \ref("P3161R6")
should return \tcode{std::wide_result},
similar to \tcode{std::widening_clmul} from \ref("P3642R7").
Wording is also provided for adding \tcode{std::widening_mul} to the working draft by itself.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

\ref("P3161R6") \q{Unified integer overflow arithmetic}
provides a number of functions that provide crucial and extremely useful numeric operations
such as \tcode{std::carrying_add} and \tcode{std::widening_mul}.

While most of these have no remaining design issues,
the return type of \tcode{std::widening_mul} in that paper is problematic
because \ref("P3642R7") \q{Carry-less product: \tcode{std::clmul}}
provides a \tcode{std::wide_result} class template specifically for the purpose
of representing the result of widening multiplication operations.
That paper is currently in LWG,
and while it uses \tcode{std::wide_result} only for \tcode{std::widening_clmul},
\tcode{std::wide_result} should also be used
for the proposed \tcode{std::widening_mul} function template.
\ref("P3161R6") still uses a \tcode{std::widening_mul_result} aggregate,
and LWG pushed back on the use of an aggregate for a variety of design reasons;
see \ref("P3642R7") for details.

To resolve this dilemma,
I propose the following direction:
\ul{
  \li{
    Change \tcode{std::wide_result} so that it accepts signed integer types
    in addition to unsigned integer types.
    The current restriction makes sense because \tcode{std::widening_clmul}
    only accepts unsigned types anyway,
    but \tcode{std::widening_mul} should obviously accept integers of any signedness.
  }
  \li{
    Either factor out \tcode{std::widening_mul} into this paper
    (i.e. delete it from \ref("P3161R6"))
    or merge the proposed changes into \ref("P3161R6").
    Merging the changes into \ref("P3161R6") would put the burden
    of modifying \tcode{std::wide_result} onto that paper's author,
    which may be cumbersome and force the author
    to become the owner of features he did not originally propose.
  }
}

\Bnote{
The other aggregate results in \ref("P3161R6") are a non-issue.
\tcode{std::carrying_add_result} pairs a \tcode{T} with a \tcode{bool} member.
These are simply two unrelated objects bundled into an aggregate,
so considerations like endianness and matching the layout of a wider integer type are irrelevant.

Similarly, \tcode{std::div_result<T>} pairs a \tcode{T} quotient and \tcode{T} remainder.
Again, these are two unrelated objects.

\tcode{std::widening_mul_result} is special because it has two members
that store the bits of a single integer value.
}

\Bnote{
For motivation, implementation experience, and more,
see \ref("P3161R6").
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054")
with the changes in \ref("P3642R7") applied.

In \eelis{version.syn}, ad a feature-test macro as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_widening_mul 20XXXXL //\serif{freestanding, also in \header{numeric}}
}
}

In \eelis{numeric.ops.overview},
move \tcode{wide_result} into a separate subclause,
and add a declaration of \tcode{widening_mul}:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
\etc

\ins{//\serif{ [numeric.wide.result], class template \tt{wide_result}}
template<class T> class wide_result;}

\ins{//\serif{ [numeric.wide.mul], function template \tt{widening_mul}}
template<class T> wide_result<T> widening_mul(T x, T y) noexcept;}

//\serif{ [numeric.clmul], carry-less product}
\del{template<class T>
class wide_result {
public:
  constexpr wide_result(T high, T low) noexcept
    : \exposid{high-bits}(high), \exposid{low-bits}(low) {}

  constexpr T high() const noexcept { return \exposid{high-bits}; }
  constexpr T low() const noexcept { return \exposid{low-bits}; }

  friend constexpr bool operator==(const wide_result&, const wide_result&) = default;
  friend constexpr strong_ordering operator<=>(const wide_result& x, const wide_result& y)
    noexcept;
private:
  T \exposid{high-bits}, \exposid{low-bits}; // \serif{exposition-only}
};}

template<class T>
  constexpr wide_result<T> widening_clmul(T x, T y) noexcept;
template<class T>
  constexpr T clmul(T x, T y) noexcept;
}
\etc
}

Remove the following from \eelis{numeric.clmul}:

\Bdel{
¶
A specialization \tcode{wide_result<T>} is ill-formed
unless \tcode{T} is an unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

¶
A specialization \tcode{wide_result<T>} is a standard-layout,
trivially copyable class type.
The members \tcode{\exposid{high-bits}} and \tcode{\exposid{low-bits}}
of any specialization of \tcode{wide_result} may be reordered.

\itemdecl{
constexpr strong_ordering operator<=>(const wide_result& x, const wide_result& y)
  noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\returns
\tcode{tie(x.\exposid{high-bits}, x.\exposid{low-bits}) <=> tie(y.\exposid{high-bits}, y.\exposid{low-bits})}.
}
}

Insert a new subclause for \tcode{wide_result} in \eelis{numeric.ops}:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Class template \tcode{wide_result} \stable_ref{numeric.wide.result}}

\itemdecl{
template<class T>
class wide_result {
public:
  using high_type = T;
  using low_type = make_unsigned_t<T>;

  constexpr wide_result(high_type high, low_type low) noexcept
    : \exposid{high-bits}(high), \exposid{low-bits}(low) {}

  constexpr high_type high() const noexcept { return \exposid{high-bits}; }
  constexpr low_type low() const noexcept { return \exposid{low-bits}; }

  friend constexpr bool operator==(const wide_result&, const wide_result&) = default;
  friend constexpr strong_ordering operator<=>(const wide_result& x, const wide_result& y)
    noexcept;
private:
  high_type \exposid{high-bits}; // \serif{exposition only}
  low_type \exposid{low-bits};   // \serif{exposition only}
};
}

¶
A specialization \tcode{wide_result<T>} is ill-formed
unless \tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

¶
A specialization \tcode{wide_result<T>} is a standard-layout,
trivially copyable class type.
The members \tcode{\exposid{high-bits}} and \tcode{\exposid{low-bits}}
of any specialization of \tcode{wide_result} may be reordered.

\itemdecl{
constexpr strong_ordering operator<=>(const wide_result& x, const wide_result& y)
  noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\returns
\tcode{tie(x.\exposid{high-bits}, x.\exposid{low-bits}) <=> tie(y.\exposid{high-bits}, y.\exposid{low-bits})}.
}
}

Insert a new subclause for \tcode{widening_mul} in \eelis{numeric.ops}:

\Bins{
\cowel_html_element("h3"){Function template \tcode{widening_mul} \stable_ref{numeric.wide.mul}}

\itemdecl{
template<class T> constexpr wide_result<T> widening_mul(T x, T y) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}.

¶
\returns
A \tcode{wide_result<T>} object storing the bits of the product of \tcode{x} and \tcode{y},
so that
\tcode{\exposid{low-bits}} stores the least significant bits of the product and
\tcode{\exposid{high-bits}} stores the subsequent bits.
}
}


